<Base title="About" wide={false}>
  <h1>About this site</h1>
  <p>I'm a final-year student at Newcastle taking CSC3132. I missed Lectures 7 and 9, half-understood Lecture 8, and realised my notes for the earlier ones were mostly copied slides that I couldn't actually use. So I rewrote the whole module properly, starting from zero, and turned it into a website so I could use it on my phone and play with the diagrams.</p>
  <p>It's not official and it's not a replacement for the lectures. If something here disagrees with the slides, the slides are right.</p>

  <h2>Why it's set up the way it is</h2>
  <p>Before building it I read a bit about how people actually learn this kind of thing, because "read the slides again" clearly wasn't working for me. The short version:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Testing yourself beats rereading.</strong> A big review of study techniques (Dunlosky et al., 2013) found practice testing and spacing your revision out worked best, and rereading and highlighting barely helped. That's why there are flashcards, quick checks in every lecture and a quiz.</li>
    <li><strong>Spacing.</strong> The flashcards use FSRS, the scheduler that Anki now uses. It brings a card back just before you'd forget it. There are only two buttons (Forgot / Remembered) because I kept overthinking the four-button version.</li>
    <li><strong>Mixing topics.</strong> Doing questions from different lectures mixed together feels worse but works better, because you have to choose the method each time. In one classroom study, mixed practice scored 72% vs 38% for doing one topic at a time (Rohrer and colleagues).</li>
    <li><strong>Guessing first.</strong> Having a go at a question before you've been taught it helps you remember the answer afterwards, even when the guess is wrong. That's the "Guess first" boxes.</li>
    <li><strong>Explaining it yourself.</strong> Writing out why each step works is one of the more effective things you can do (Bisra et al., 2018). Worked examples help most at the start and less once you know it, which is why the exercises have a switch to hide the working.</li>
    <li><strong>Pictures next to the maths.</strong> α is always purple and β always teal, in the equations and in the diagrams, so you can follow them through.</li>
    <li><strong>Recall cards inside the notes.</strong> I stole this idea from <a href="https://quantum.country">Quantum Country</a> by Andy Matuschak and Michael Nielsen, which puts spaced-repetition questions in the middle of the explanation.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How it's built</h2>
  <p>It's a static site made with <a href="https://astro.build">Astro</a>, hosted on Cloudflare Pages. The diagrams are plain SVG and TypeScript, the maths is KaTeX, and the code lab runs on a small state-vector simulator I can test with <code>npm test</code>. Nothing is sent anywhere: your progress lives in your own browser.</p>
</Base>
